{number: 23}
{title: It Came Upon the Midnight Clear}
{key: Bb}

{start_of_verse: 1}
[Bb] It came upon the [Eb] midnight clear,
That [Bb] glorious song of [F] old,
From [Bb] angels bending [Eb] near the earth,
To [Bb] touch their [F7] harps of [Bb] gold:
"Peace [D7] on the earth, good-[Gm] will to men,
From [F] heaven's all gracious [C7] King": [F]
The [Bb] world in solemn [Eb] stillness lay,
To [Bb] hear the [F7] angels [Bb] sing.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 2}
Still [Bb] through the cloven [Eb] skies they come
With [Bb] peaceful wings un-[F] furled,
And [Bb] still their heavenly [Eb] music floats
O'er [Bb] all the [F7] weary [Bb] world;
A-[D7] bove its sad and [Gm] lowly plains,
They [F] bend on hovering [C7] wing, [F]
And [Bb] ever o'er its [Eb] Babel sounds
The [Bb] blessed [F7] angels [Bb] sing.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 3}
And [Bb] ye, beneath life's [Eb] crushing load,
Whose [Bb] forms are bending [F] low,
Who [Bb] toil along the [Eb] climbing way
With [Bb] painful [F7] steps and [Bb] slow,
Look [D7] now! for glad and [Gm] golden hours
Come [F] swiftly on the [C7] wing; [F]
O [Bb] rest beside the [Eb] weary road,
And [Bb] hear the [F7] angels [Bb] sing.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 4}
For [Bb] lo, the days are [Eb] hastening on,
By [Bb] prophet bards fore-[F] told,
When [Bb] with the ever-[Eb] circling years
Comes [Bb] round the [F7] age of [Bb] gold;
When [D7] peace shall over [Gm] all the earth
Its [F] ancient splendors [C7] fling, [F]
And [Bb] the whole world give [Eb] back the song
Which [Bb] now the [F7] angels [Bb] sing.
{end_of_verse}
